'''Grup Llibertat, S.C.''' ({{lang|ca|Grup Llibertat}}), commonly referred to as '''Llibertat''', is a Balboan worker cooperative and the country's largest agricultural enterprise. Headquartered in [[Sant Bart]], [[Jala Province]], the cooperative owns and operates former plantation lands across the Balboan interior, producing sugar, coffee, bananas, cacao, tropical fruits, and cotton for export and domestic consumption.

Grup Llibertat was formed in 1964 during the BPP government's post-reunification land reform programme, when the [[Balboan Land and Reparations Authority]] expropriated large plantations owned by white Balboan families and reorganised them as agricultural cooperatives. The cooperative's name — ''llibertat'' meaning "freedom" in Volisanian — was chosen by the former plantation workers who became its founding members, reflecting the significance of the land reform as an act of economic emancipation after centuries of coerced labour.

The cooperative is structured as a worker-owned enterprise: its founding members and their descendants hold the majority of shares, and the cooperative's governance is based on democratic principles, with each worker-member entitled to one vote regardless of their shareholding. Grup Llibertat is one of the largest worker cooperatives in Abyala and a model for the cooperative agricultural sector that replaced the plantation economy in the Balboan interior.

== History ==

=== Land reform and founding (1962–1964) ===

{{main|Balboan Land and Reparations Authority}}

The [[Twelve Guarantees]], accepted by the Balboan government in 1960 as a condition for the return of the Sant Cristòfor mandate, required the dismantling of the racial state and the implementation of economic reforms, including reparations to descendants of enslaved persons. The [[Balboan Land and Reparations Authority]] (BLRA) was established in 1962 to administer the expropriation of the large plantations that had been the economic foundation of the white planter oligarchy since the colonial period.

The expropriation was one of the most significant and controversial policies of the BPP government. The plantations — which occupied the most fertile land in the Jala and Migjorn highlands and the Aethiopian coastal plain — had been owned by white Balboan families for generations, and their expropriation was resisted by the planter class through legal challenges, political pressure, and sporadic violence. The BLRA, however, was empowered by the Twelve Guarantees framework, which provided AC enforcement mechanisms, and the expropriation proceeded despite the opposition.

The BLRA's approach was to reorganise the expropriated plantations as agricultural cooperatives, to which former plantation workers were granted shares. The model was designed to preserve the productive capacity of the land while transferring ownership from the planter class to the workers who had cultivated it. The cooperatives were given initial capital through the reparations programme, technical assistance from the AC, and preferential access to government agricultural credit.

Grup Llibertat was formed in 1964 as the consolidation of twelve expropriated plantations in the Jala highlands — the largest single cooperative created by the land reform programme. The founding assembly, held in Sant Bart on 15 March 1964, was attended by over 5,000 former plantation workers, who voted to adopt the cooperative structure and elected the first governing council. The name ''Llibertat'' was chosen at the founding assembly, proposed by a group of Black Balboan women who had been sharecroppers on the largest of the expropriated plantations.

=== Expansion (1964–1980) ===

The cooperative's early years were marked by the challenges of transitioning from a plantation economy — in which the workers had been labourers under the direction of white managers — to a cooperative enterprise in which the workers themselves were responsible for management, marketing, and investment decisions. The BLRA provided technical assistance and training programmes, and the AC funded agricultural extension services that helped the cooperative develop its management capacity.

The cooperative expanded through the 1970s as additional plantations were expropriated and their workers voted to join Grup Llibertat rather than form separate cooperatives. By 1975, the cooperative encompassed over thirty former plantations across Jala and Migjorn provinces, with a membership of approximately 15,000 worker-owners. The expansion was driven by the economic advantages of scale: the cooperative could negotiate better prices for its produce, access cheaper credit, and invest in processing facilities that smaller cooperatives could not afford.

The [[1968–1978 Balboa recession]] tested the cooperative's resilience. The recession reduced demand for Balboan agricultural exports and tightened the credit available from the domestic banking sector. Grup Llibertat, however, was better positioned than many smaller cooperatives: its scale allowed it to maintain production through the downturn, and its access to the AC-funded agricultural credit programme provided a financial buffer that smaller enterprises lacked. The cooperative emerged from the recession with a larger market share, as smaller cooperatives that had failed were absorbed into its operations.

=== Modern era (1980–present) ===

The [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis]] of the late 1980s had a limited direct impact on Grup Llibertat — the cooperative's operations were in the interior, far from the speculative real-estate market of the Estret corridor — but the broader economic contraction reduced demand for agricultural commodities and the cooperative's revenue declined through the early 1990s. The cooperative responded by diversifying its product range, investing in processing facilities that added value to its raw agricultural output, and expanding its export markets beyond the traditional Volisanian and Illypnian destinations.

Capital for modernisation and expansion was raised through member contributions, cooperative credit facilities, and a long-term investment from the [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]], which acquired a 10 per cent stake in 2001. The cooperative remained privately held throughout this period, with ownership restricted to its worker-members and a small number of institutional investors.

The 2000s and 2010s saw the cooperative invest in organic and fair-trade certification, which commanded premium prices in the Illypnian and Abyalan markets. The cooperative also expanded its domestic processing capacity, establishing sugar refineries, coffee roasteries, and fruit-packing facilities in Sant Bart and the Jala highlands. By 2026, Grup Llibertat employed approximately 28,000 workers — of whom approximately 20,000 were worker-members — and was the largest agricultural enterprise in Balboa.

== Structure and governance ==

Grup Llibertat is structured as a worker cooperative under Balboan cooperative law. The cooperative's governing document, the ''Estatuts de Llibertat'', establishes the principles of democratic governance, equitable distribution of surplus, and community investment that guide its operations.

The cooperative's governance structure consists of:

* The '''General Assembly''', comprising all worker-members, which meets annually to approve the cooperative's accounts, elect the governing council, and vote on major strategic decisions. Each worker-member has one vote, regardless of their position within the cooperative.
* The '''Governing Council''', elected by the General Assembly, which oversees the cooperative's management and sets its strategic direction. The council consists of twelve members elected for three-year terms.
* The '''General Manager''', appointed by the Governing Council, who is responsible for the cooperative's day-to-day operations.

The cooperative distributes its surplus to worker-members in proportion to their labour contribution, after retaining a portion for reinvestment and community development. The ''Estatuts'' require that at least 10 per cent of the annual surplus be allocated to community development programmes in the cooperative's operating areas, including education, healthcare, and infrastructure.

== Operations ==

Grup Llibertat operates across three provinces in the Balboan interior:

* '''Jala Province''': The cooperative's principal operations are in the Jala highlands, where it cultivates coffee, cacao, and tropical fruits on the former plantation lands. The Jala operations account for approximately 55 per cent of the cooperative's revenue.
* '''Migjorn Province''': The cooperative operates sugar and banana plantations in the Migjorn lowlands and the Aethiopian coastal plain. The Migjorn operations account for approximately 30 per cent of revenue.
* '''Tramuntana Province''': The cooperative operates cotton and mixed-crop farms in the northern highlands. The Tramuntana operations account for approximately 15 per cent of revenue.

The cooperative also operates processing facilities in Sant Bart and the Jala highlands, including sugar refineries, coffee roasteries, fruit-packing plants, and cotton gins. The processing operations add value to the cooperative's raw agricultural output and provide employment in the interior towns.

== Controversies ==

=== White Balboan land claims ===

The expropriation of the plantations that formed Grup Llibertat remains contested by some white Balboan families, who argue that the compensation offered by the BLRA was below market value and that the expropriation was a violation of property rights. The claims have been pursued through the Balboan courts and the AC Tribunal, but have been consistently rejected on the grounds that the expropriation was conducted in accordance with the Twelve Guarantees framework and that the compensation was fair.

=== Labour practices ===

Despite its cooperative structure, Grup Llibertat has faced criticism over its treatment of non-member workers — seasonal labourers and contract workers who are not entitled to shares in the cooperative. Critics argue that the cooperative's dual workforce, in which worker-members enjoy significantly better wages and benefits than non-member workers, creates a two-tier labour system that undermines the cooperative's egalitarian principles. The cooperative has responded by expanding its membership programme and increasing the proportion of its workforce that holds shares, but non-member workers continue to account for a significant portion of its labour force.

